  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 136 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nPWATs continue climbing Sunday and Monday with increasing coverage \nof showers and storms expected, especially for central and western \nNM. Forecast confidence on the coverage of storms with beneficial \nrainfall is still low to moderate. The GFS and ECMWF both show a \nweak ridge extending westward into southern NM from the main upper \nhigh farther east over TX. This pattern may choke off the deeper \nmoisture and lead to greater stability over parts of the area. An \nupper level trough approaching from the west may provide larger-\nscale ascent to overcome any stability issues by Monday.\n\nThe rest of next week looks downright wet with surprisingly good \nconsensus from extended models. The upper high shifts into the \nsoutheast U.S. while an upper level low/trough develops along the \nWest Coast. PWATs are also shown soaring to near record values for \nmid September. Widespread rainfall amounts between 0.50\" and 1.50\" \nare shown during the 4-day period from Monday to Thursday on the \nlatest NBM with 90th percentile amounts >3\". Given the lackluster\nperformance of monsoon bursts this summer, forecast confidence \nwill remain low to moderate until we get much closer.",
